Poster 362
Osmotic Demyelination in Lyme Disease: A
Case Report
Arpit
Arora, MD (SUNY Downstate Medical Center at Brooklyn, Old
Bridge, New Jersey, United States), Necolle Morgado-Vega, DO
Disclosures: Arpit Arora: I Have No Relevant Financial Relationships
To Disclose
Case/Program Description: This is a patient with a history of colon
cancer, status post partial colon resection, who presented to our
institution with a one-week history of bilateral upper extremity
weakness, lethargy, diffuse myalgias, and decreased appetite after
starting losartan. He attributed these to the new medication and
stopped taking it; the symptoms persisted, however, and he pre-
sented to the ED. The patient was noted to have SIADH as
demonstrated by hyponatremia (N a 118), hypo-osmolar serum, and
hyperosmolar urine. He was started on ﬂuid restriction with minor
improvement in sodium levels. He was also evaluated by Neurology
for his upper extremity weakness; workup included MRI of the
brain, which showed osmotic demyelination of the midbrain. The
patient was subsequently admitted to the acute inpatient reha-
bilitation service. His hyponatremia slowly improved but weakness
persisted.
Setting: Inpatient rehabilitation unit at tertiary care hospital.
Results: Neurology follow-up was obtained. Further workup included
a Lyme titer, which came back positive. The patient was started on IV
ceftriaxone as per an ID consultation. Over the next week, the pa-
tient’s sodium and weakness improved signiﬁcantly. The patient was
diagnosed with central pontine myelinolysis secondary to Lyme
disease.
Discussion: Lyme disease and hyponatremia can both cause muscle
weakness, but Lyme disease as the etiology of SIADH is less common.
Conclusions: In endemic areas, Lyme disease should be considered as
an etiology of SIADH.
Level of Evidence: Level V
Poster 363
Primary Lymphoma in Immunocompetent Man
Masquerading as CVA: A Case Report
Arpit Arora, MD (SUNY Downstate Medical Center at Brooklyn, Old
Bridge, New Jersey, United States), Caitlin Cicone, DO,
Necolle Morgado-Vega, DO
Disclosures: Arpit Arora: I Have No Relevant Financial Relationships
To Disclose
Case/Program Description: This is a patient with diabetes, diabetic
neuropathy, hypertension, hyperlipidemia and no known immuno-
compromising state who presented to the outpatient Rehabilitation
clinic for Physical Therapy evaluation. The patient originally
started having nausea and dizziness approximately 2 months prior
to admission and stopped taking Cymbalta at his Neurologist’s
recommendation. The symptoms did not subside and he later
developed diplopia causing him to go to the ED. A non-contrast
head CT revealed no abnormalities and the patient was discharged
home with outpatient follow-up. The patient’s diplopia, however,
continued to worsen and an MRI was obtained which showed a
right medullary CVA. The patient was then referred for outpatient
therapy in our clinic. During his examination by the attending
physiatrist, he was deemed to be a good candidate for acute
inpatient rehabilitation and admitted to the service. The patient’s
diplopia and gait dysfunction initially remained stable. Over the
next few days, however, he noted worsening ataxia and diplopia
and developed dysphagia. An MRI was ordered which showed large
masses in the third and fourth ventricles with surrounding edema
in the brainstem and cerebellum. Neurology, Hematology/
Oncology, Neurosurgery, and Radiation Oncology consults were
obtained. The patient was transferred to the Medicine service for
further care.
Setting: Inpatient rehabilitation unit at tertiary care hospital.
Results: The patient underwent a brain biopsy and started on IV
steroids. He was diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma diagnosed by
biopsy (PCNSL).
Discussion: PCNSL is traditionally thought of as a disease
affecting primarily immunocompro mised individuals, however the
incidence in immunocompetent individuals has been steadily
increasing over the past several years. This case demonstrates the
importance of keeping a broad differential when establishing a
diagnosis.
